# Tailgunner sound board -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Cinematronics vector games carry a small audio board of their own, driven from the
# CPU board's output latch: the latch bits arrive here as ports (OUT_0 ... OUT_7, MAME's
# TTL_INPUT names), and the board's sound goes out to a final amplifier that MAME does
# not simulate and this file therefore does not contain.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/cinematronics/nl_tailg.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(tailg). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Aaron Giles (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/cinematronics/nl_tailg.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
# The sheet numbers its ICs IC1, IC2 ...; they are written U1, U2 ... here, because the
# checker files every IC under a U designator.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 30: #define HLE_NOISE_GEN 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 31: #define HLE_HYPERSPACE_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 32: #define HLE_LASER_VCO 1 -> 0 (high-level stand-in for a real circuit)
# line 33: #define REMOVE_LS123_DIODES 1 -> 0 (parts removed for speed)
# line 34: #define ADD_LS125_PULLUPS 1 -> 0 (pull-ups the board does not have)
# line 670: dropped NET_C(R70.2, GND) -- the sheet takes R70 on to IC19 pin 2, the final amplifier's summing junction (C34, R74 and the pot R75 as feedback); MAME grounds it instead
# line 676: dropped NET_C(GND, IC19.2, IC19.3) -- took IC19.2 out of it: the sheet grounds IC19 pin 3 only; pin 2 is the final amplifier's summing junction
# line 677: NET_C(GND, IC5.9, IC5.11, IC5.13, IC6.9, IC6.11, IC6.13) -> nc -- tied to GND in MAME's "Unconnected inputs" block
# line 695: MAME ties U23.11 off as unused, but the circuit uses them -- the tie is dropped
# Declarations MAME comments out but the restored circuit uses, restored: Q1 (line 208), Q2 (line 209)
#
# Corrected from the sheet (the notes below say what was read where):
#   p55: R70 into IC19 pin 2: R70.2 U19.2
#   p55: IC19 pin 6 drives the output stage: U19.6 (FINAL_AMP)
#   p55: IC23 pin 11 (Q6) leaves the board at J3-9, 'TO START BUTTON LIGHT': U23.11 (START_LIGHT)
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_0), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_1), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_2), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_3), TTL_INPUT(I_OUT_4)
#
# Left out by MAME (commented out at TAG, so not in this file either):
#   line 137: RES(R71, 150)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 138: RES(R72, RES_K(22))  -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 139: RES(R73, 150)        -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 140: RES(R74, RES_K(47))  -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 141: POT(R75, RES_K(100)) -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 186: CAP(C34, CAP_P(470)) -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 187: CAP(C35, CAP_P(470)) -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 188: CAP(C36, CAP_P(470)) -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 189: CAP(C37, CAP_U(3.3))
#   line 190: CAP(C38, CAP_U(3.3))
#   line 191: CAP(C39, CAP_U(3.3))
#   line 192: CAP(C40, CAP_U(3.3))
#   line 208: Q_2N3904(Q1)            // NPN -- not used
#   line 209: Q_2N3904(Q2)            // NPN -- not used
#   line 213: Q_2N6292(Q6)            // NPN -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 214: Q_2N6107(Q7)            // PNP -- part of final amp (not emulated)
#   line 285: TTL_7915_DIP(IC20)      // -15V Regulator -- not needed
#   line 286: TTL_7815_DIP(IC21)      // +15V Regulator -- not needed
#
# Two MAME changes are undone by their switches: the diodes D8/D9 on the 74LS123's
# Rext/Cext pins are back (MAME snips them for speed), and the pull-ups MAME adds to the
# 74LS125 outputs are gone. The hyperspace VCO's real circuit uses Q1, which MAME declares
# only in a comment ('not used' -- by the stand-in); the declaration is restored.
#
# Compared with sheet 1 of the audio board, Schematic Audio Board - Tailgunner SD205202 (doc
# 22a6fb78dd5a p55, 600 dpi), and the parts list PL205202 (pp41-43), 2026-10-20: the trigger
# port IC23 with its J4 inputs (O0-O4 on J4-11 to J4-15) and its six used outputs; the noise
# generator (D1, IC1-IC3, but see below); the explosion (IC9, IC10, IC12, Q3), rumble (IC6,
# Q4) and shield (IC4, Q5, IC13, the 555s IC16 and IC17 with their RC ladders); the shield
# bounce (IC14, IC15, IC18, IC12, Q8, IC22); the hyperspace and laser VCOs (IC7/Q1,
# IC11/Q2) with the 74LS393 IC8 and its taps R17-R20 and R35-R38; and the summing line into
# R70 all agree with MAME pin for pin and value for value, diode and transistor orientation
# included, and every value MAME has (R1-R70, R76-R84, C1-C33, C41-C44) agrees with the parts
# list. The three MAME changes this file undoes are what the sheet draws: D8 and D9 on the
# 74LS123's pins 15 and 7, no pull-ups on the 74LS125 outputs (pins 3 and 6), and the
# hyperspace VCO built from IC7 and Q1 into IC5 pin 1. Corrected from the sheet: IC23 pin 11
# (Q6), which MAME leaves unconnected, goes to J3-9 "TO START BUTTON LIGHT"; and R70 goes
# into IC19 pin 2, the final amplifier's inverting input, rather than to ground, with IC19
# pin 3 grounded (MAME lines 670 and 676).
#
# Open question, as printed and not settled:
# * The sheet draws the noise amplifier IC1 with C3/R2 on pin 2 (-) and R4/C4 on pin 3 (+),
#   and MAME copies it (its lines 325 and 328, in the branch it never runs). As drawn R4 is
#   positive feedback and the op-amp would latch; the manual's own text (p24) gives IC1 a
#   gain of 1000, which is 1 + R4/R3 only with R4 on the inverting input; and Sundance's
#   sheet SD203200, the same circuit by the same drafter, draws C3 into pin 3 and the
#   feedback into pin 2. This file follows the sheet. Not checked against a board.
#
# Slips in the parts list that change nothing here: item 32 gives R22 as 22K (R22 is 1K on
# the sheet and in item 24; the 22K is the final amplifier's R72), and items 14 and 18 garble
# C45-C49. The theory text says Q5 for the rumble transistor (the sheet: Q4) and Q1 for the
# laser squarer (Q2). IC1's printed type is unreadable on this scan; the parts list gives
# TL081. Not in this file: sheet 2 (p65, the joystick circuitry on the same board, IC24-IC28),
# and the final amplifier and regulators (R71-R75, C34-C40, Q6, Q7, IC20, IC21), whose values
# agree with MAME's commented-out lines.

